<IndustryPage {...pageData}>
  <p>Transport and haulage companies operate in one of the most regulated industries in the UK. Driver hours, tachograph analysis, O-licence compliance, vehicle maintenance, and DVSA reporting are non-negotiable - get them wrong and you face fines, licence suspension, or worse. Yet most SME haulage operators manage this complexity with a patchwork of generic software, spreadsheets, and paper.</p>

  <p>The typical transport tech stack is a generic fleet tracking tool, a separate accounting system, a tachograph analysis tool that nobody enjoys using, and a job management process that lives in WhatsApp groups and whiteboards. Nothing connects. Route planning is done by the transport manager's gut feel. Proof of delivery is paper slips that get lost. Customer queries about delivery status are answered by phone calls that take the dispatcher away from actually managing the fleet.</p>

  <p>Off-the-shelf transport software exists, but it is built for large operators with dedicated IT teams. SMEs get the "lite" version - fewer features, no customisation, and a per-vehicle subscription that adds up fast. A 20-vehicle fleet can easily spend £15,000-£25,000 a year on SaaS subscriptions that almost fit. And none of it integrates with your accounting, your telematics, or your customer communication.</p>

  <p>Bespoke software solves this by starting with your operation. Your fleet management system knows what an O-licence is and tracks it. Your tachograph compliance is built in, not bolted on - driver hours are tracked in real-time, breaches are flagged before they happen, and DVSA reports are generated with one click. Route planning considers your actual constraints - vehicle types, driver hours, delivery windows, and customer priorities - not generic algorithms that ignore the realities of UK roads.</p>

  <p>The driver app is where the day-to-day impact is biggest. Drivers get job details, routes, and delivery instructions on their phone. They capture proof of delivery with a photo and signature. They update job status with one tap. No paper, no phone calls to base, no lost POD slips. And when signal drops on a rural delivery route, the app keeps working - syncing data when connectivity returns.</p>

  <p>A UK haulage operator with 18 vehicles was spending £18,000 a year across three SaaS tools - fleet tracking, tachograph analysis, and a basic job management system. None integrated. Tacho analysis took the transport manager half a day a week. We built a single platform covering fleet management, tacho compliance, job tracking, driver app, and customer portal with telematics integration. Build cost: £35,000. Tacho processing dropped from 4 hours a week to 20 minutes. Empty running reduced by 12% through better route planning. The operator owns the software outright - hosting costs £60/month.</p>

  <p>If your transport operation is spending more than £10,000 a year on software that does not quite fit, <a href="/services">explore our services</a> or <a href="/contact">get in touch</a> to discuss whether bespoke software makes sense for your fleet.</p>
